{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And they were a bitterness of spirit to Yitzchak and to Rivkah.</b> Desire and will he calls in our language 'spirit' (רוח), in the language of the Rabbis, that one is able to walk against the spirit of each and every person; and the verse says of these women that each one was rebellious of spirit toward Yitzchak and Rivkah, that is to say, she would anger them and do everything she did contrary to their will." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'Morat ruach' — raising the spirit, removing the holy spirit (רוח הקודש) from Yitzchak and Rivkah. And the matter comes because the holy spirit departs for two reasons: the first, because of angers and troubles that come upon a person, and as a result he is sad, and the holy spirit does not dwell amidst sadness but amidst a matter of joy; and the second, because of the company of the wicked, as in the matter written concerning Avraham (Genesis 13:14), 'after Lot separated from him.' And the Rabbis of blessed memory said that Rivkah was a daughter of priests and was not scrupulous about the filth of idolatry, while Yitzchak, because he was a son of holy ones, was scrupulous about the filth of idolatry — therefore it says <b>to Yitzchak</b> first."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:30.654363650Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}